BBUUGGSS
   HHiissttoorriiccaall bbuuggss ((nnooww ffiixxeedd))
       Before glibc 2.28, on some architectures (e.g., x86‐64) ppooww() may be
       more than 10,000 times slower for some inputs than for other nearby
       inputs.  This affects only ppooww(), and not ppoowwff() nor ppoowwll().  This
       problem was fixed in glibc 2.28.

       A number of bugs in the glibc implementation of ppooww() were fixed in
       glibc 2.16.

       In glibc 2.9 and earlier, when a pole error occurs, _e_r_r_n_o is set to
       EEDDOOMM instead of the POSIX‐mandated EERRAANNGGEE.  Since glibc 2.10, glibc
       does the right thing.

       In glibc 2.3.2 and earlier, when an overflow or underflow error occurs,
       glibc’s ppooww() generates a bogus invalid floating‐point exception
       (FFEE__IINNVVAALLIIDD) in addition to the overflow or underflow exception.

